BHARATPATH — QUESTIONS FOR THE CLIENT
Prepared 27 August 2026, after the document comments and the note received today.

WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
--------------------
We have applied everything that was clear to the build plan. This file lists only the
things we could not act on, because the answer changes what we build. Thirteen questions.

Three of them stop work if they are not answered:
  Q1  and  Q2  — we cannot build the scoring engine (needed by Day 8)
  Q8           — we cannot build the employer unlock (needed by Day 14)

Everything else we can start on and finish later, but the sooner they land the less
rework there is.

Each question below has: what you told us, why we are stuck, and what we need back.
Written plainly so it can be read out on a call or pasted into the group.


================================================================================
GROUP A — THE SCORE
The four questions here are all about the same thing: the new numbers do not add up,
and we do not want to guess.
================================================================================

Q1. CAN YOU WALK US THROUGH THE SCORE WITH ONE REAL EXAMPLE?   [BLOCKS DAY 8]

  You told us:  "Base - 700, max - 990 ... critically judge and give points out of
                200 points (990-790)"

  Where we are stuck:
    Those numbers do not fit together. If everyone starts at 700, and the judged part
    is worth 200 points, the highest anyone can reach is 900 — not 990.
    But you have also written the judged part as running from 790 to 990, which
    suggests something else lifts a candidate from 700 up to 790 first, and nobody
    has told us what that is.

    So there is a 90-point gap we cannot explain:

         700 ............ 790 =============================== 990
          ^                ^                                    ^
        base       what fills this?              the 200 points you described

  What we need:
    Take one real CV. Tell us the number it should come out with, and show us how you
    got there step by step. One worked example answers this faster than any amount of
    written explanation.


Q2. IS THE 200-POINT PART SCORED BY AN AI, OR BY FIXED RULES?   [BLOCKS DAY 8]

  You told us:  "Critically judge and give points out of 200 points"

  Why we are asking:
    "Critically judge" sounds like an AI reading the CV and forming an opinion, rather
    than a fixed formula. Both are possible to build. But they are built very
    differently, so we need to know before we start.

    The reason this matters: you have asked that the score be reproducible — the same
    CV must always give the same number. A fixed formula does that naturally. An AI
    does not; ask it twice and you can get two answers.

    We can still make an AI-based score reproducible, but only by locking the AI
    version, saving its exact answer against the score, and replaying that saved
    answer instead of asking again. That is a real design and we will build it
    properly — we just cannot start it while it might be the wrong design.

  What we need:
    Just tell us which one. If it is an AI, we will design for it.


Q3. IS THE SCORE EXPLAINED TO THE CANDIDATE, OR NOT?

  You told us two different things:
    In the document:  "Score is not explainable but is reproducible"
    In the note:      "critically judge and give points out of 200 points,
                       explaination required"

  Why we are asking:
    The first says do not show people how the number was reached. The second says an
    explanation is required. These cannot both be built.

    It also matters because the original requirement document promises the candidate a
    category-by-category breakdown and improvement tips on the results screen. If the
    score is no longer explained, that screen needs to change.

  What we need:
    Tell us what the candidate actually sees after their CV is scored:
      (a) the number only
      (b) the number plus a written comment on their CV, but no formula
      (c) the number plus a full breakdown by category, as originally specified

    Our guess is (b) — no formula shown, but a written reason. Confirm or correct.


Q4. ARE THE COURSE AND INTERVIEW POINTS PER ITEM, OR A ONE-TIME TOTAL?

  You told us:  "Course/Videos - Add 30 to the score"
                "Mock Interview - Per Session 20 points increase, up to 60 points"

  Why we are asking:
    The interview one is clear: 20 points each time, three times maximum, 60 total.
    The course one is not. If a candidate finishes five courses, is that 30 points or
    150 points?

  What we need, three short answers:
    - Is +30 per course, or +30 no matter how many courses they do?
    - If it is per course, is there a maximum?
    - If a subscription lapses and is renewed, do the points reset, or are they kept
      permanently once earned?


Q5. DO THE EXTRA POINTS STOP AT 990?

  Why we are asking:
    If a candidate's CV already scores 985 and they then complete a course worth 30
    points, do they end up at 990 (capped) or at 1015 (above your stated maximum)?

  What we need:
    One line. Our assumption is that nothing ever goes above 990 — confirm.


================================================================================
GROUP B — MONEY AND ACCESS
================================================================================

Q6. IS THE SCORE ITSELF BEHIND THE PAYWALL?

  You told us:  "Only Sign Up is allowed, nothing else, not even the resume upload,
                 paid first audience only"
                "Student -> Subscription (Nothing free)"

  Why we are asking:
    Read together, these say: a person signs up, then has to pay, and only then can
    they upload their CV and see a score.

    We can build that. But we want to say plainly that it is a significant change of
    direction. The original plan was that the free score is what brings people to the
    platform — they see their number, they are impressed, and then they buy. If the
    score is now behind payment, the platform is asking people to pay before they have
    seen anything, which is a much harder sell and changes how the app is marketed.

    This is your business decision, not ours. We only want it stated clearly rather
    than assumed.

  What we need:
    Confirm the order:
      (a) sign up -> pay -> upload CV -> see score        (what we have understood)
      (b) sign up -> upload CV -> see score free -> pay for courses and interviews
      (c) something else


Q7. KYB — AUTOMATIC, OR MANUAL?

  You told us two different things, three days apart:
    24 August, on the doc:  "Manual, uploading will be there but manual"
    27 August, in the note: "KYB won't be there, automatic approval"

  What we have done in the meantime:
    We have built it so that both work. The full verification process exists in the
    system, but there is a single setting that currently approves every employer
    automatically. Turning real verification back on later is a settings change, not
    a rebuild. That costs us an hour now and protects you either way.

  One thing we must put on record:
    With automatic approval, any business that signs up can post jobs and pay to see
    candidates' phone numbers and email addresses, with no check that the business is
    real. That is a fraud risk and a risk to your candidates' personal data. Your own
    requirement documents currently say verification is mandatory before a job goes
    live. We are comfortable building it your way — we just need you to have decided
    it knowingly.

  What we need:
    Confirm: automatic approval for now, and we keep the switch. Or tell us manual
    review is back, and we turn it on.


Q8. WHAT DOES AN EMPLOYER TIER ACTUALLY INCLUDE?   [BLOCKS DAY 14]

  You asked this yourself in the document, and it was not answered:
    "How will the unlock work, subscription based? allowing 50 unlocks at once under
     tiers or one at a time."

  Why we are asking:
    This is the single biggest open item on the employer side. The two options are
    entirely different systems:

      Option A — the tier includes a bundle. An employer pays for a tier and gets, say,
                 50 unlocks. We count down from 50. When it hits zero they upgrade.

      Option B — the tier is an access fee. The employer pays a monthly fee to use the
                 platform, and then pays separately each time they unlock a candidate.

      Option C — both. A tier includes 50, and they can buy more on top.

  What we need:
    Which option, and the numbers. Also: if an employer unlocks the same candidate
    twice, are they charged twice? (We have assumed no — once unlocked, always
    unlocked, charged once.)


Q9. PLEASE SEND THE EMPLOYER TYPE AND INDUSTRY LISTS.

  You told us:  "Employer - MNC, Industry"

  Why we are asking:
    We have added both fields. But they have to be a fixed list of choices, not a
    free-typing box — otherwise one employer types "Manufacturing", another types
    "manufacturing ltd", and no filter or pricing rule will ever work properly.

  What we need:
    - The full list of employer types. Is it just MNC and non-MNC? Or MNC / Large /
      SME / Startup, or something else?
    - The full list of industries. Twenty to thirty options is normal.
    - Does the employer type affect what they pay? (We suspect it does, given the
      "Flat Fee / Tier" model.)


Q10. COLLEGE SEATS — WHAT HAPPENS WHEN THEY RUN OUT?

  You told us:  "College -> B2B Deal, No of Seats will be assigned from the admin.
                 (CRUD Operations and stop operations in backend admin)"

  Why we are asking:
    We have built seat allocation and an admin control to stop a college. Two things
    are not defined:

    First, if a college is given 500 seats and uploads 600 students, what should
    happen? Block the extra 100? Let them all in and bill the college for 600? Let
    them in but only show analytics for 500?

    Second, when your admin uses "stop operations" on a college, what exactly stops?
    Only the college staff losing access to their dashboard? Or do the students who
    joined through that college lose their accounts too? We have assumed the college
    staff only, and students keep their own accounts — please confirm, because the
    other reading would cut off real candidates.

  What we need:
    An answer to both.


Q11. DO SUBSCRIPTIONS RENEW AUTOMATICALLY?

  You told us:  "Subscription ... Per month/quarterly/semester/annually"

  Why we are asking:
    There are two ways to do this in India and the difference is large.

      Manual — when the month ends, the candidate gets a reminder and pays again
               themselves. Simple. We can build this quickly.

      Automatic — the money is taken automatically each month. In India this requires
               setting up a UPI mandate with each individual user first, which is a
               separate approval flow with its own rules and limits. It is more work,
               it needs the payment gateway to support it, and it needs to be part of
               the sign-up experience.

    Automatic renewal keeps far more customers. Manual is cheaper to build. It is
    worth a proper decision, not a default.

  What we need:
    Which one for launch. If automatic, we also need to confirm your payment gateway
    supports recurring UPI mandates before we design around it.


Q12. WE NEED THREE CHANGES CONFIRMED IN WRITING.

  Why we are asking:
    This is not us being difficult. Your specification documents list a set of things
    the finished build will be checked against — that is the list we get measured on
    at handover. Three of your new instructions contradict that list. If we simply
    follow the new instruction, the build technically fails its own acceptance test,
    and that becomes an argument at the end of the project rather than now.

    A one-line email or a WhatsApp message from you is enough. We are not asking for
    a formal document.

  The three items:

    1. Paid add-ons now change the score.
       Your rule 3 currently says paid add-ons must NEVER change the core score, and
       that this must be provable. Your comment "It will change it" reverses that.
       We need that reversal confirmed.

    2. Sign-up is required before anything.
       Your specification lists "Candidate can reach the first score before mandatory
       account creation" as something the build must do. Your new instruction is the
       opposite. We need the old requirement formally withdrawn.

    3. Duplicate CV detection is dropped.
       Your requirements name duplicate and copied CVs as the main thing the integrity
       system should catch. Your comment says "Don't flag for duplicates". Confirm we
       are removing it. (To be clear: we are NOT removing the check that stops one
       candidate applying to the same job twice — that is unrelated and it stays.)


Q13. WHAT HAPPENS TO SOMEONE'S DATA WHEN THEY ASK TO BE DELETED?
     [This one needs your lawyer, and lawyers take time — please start it now.]

  Why we are asking:
    Three of your own requirements pull against each other:
      - Users must be able to have their data deleted on request.
      - The audit trail can never be altered or deleted.
      - Payment and subscription records have to be kept by law for several years.

    So when a candidate asks to be deleted, we cannot delete everything, and we cannot
    keep everything. Someone has to decide the line — and that someone is a lawyer,
    not us. We should not be inventing your data retention policy.

  What we need:
    A short written policy answering:
      - Which details are permanently erased (name, phone, email, CV file)?
      - Which are kept but anonymised, so records still add up?
      - How long are payment and subscription records kept?
      - What stays in the audit trail after a user is deleted?

    We need this by roughly three weeks in. Given legal review takes time, please
    start it this week.


================================================================================
ONE THING WE WANT TO RAISE, SEPARATE FROM THE QUESTIONS
================================================================================

Your own requirements ban any wording that makes this look like a credit score, and
they describe this as a legal requirement rather than a preference. The reason is
obvious once said out loud: a three-digit number shown to Indian consumers looks
exactly like a CIBIL score.

The new instructions change the shape of that risk. The product now sells something
that provably increases that number, and the number itself sits behind a paid
subscription. "Pay us and your score goes up" is a harder thing to defend than
"here is your score, free".

We are not refusing to build it, and we are not lawyers. But if your counsel signed
off on the score at any point, they signed off on a different product to this one.
Please have them look again before we build the scoring engine — that is roughly two
weeks away. It costs a day now. It costs a rebuild later.


================================================================================
WHAT WE HAVE ALREADY ACTIONED — NO REPLY NEEDED
================================================================================

These were clear, and the build plan has been updated already:

  - Paid add-ons now raise the score. Course +30, interview +20 per session up to +60.
    We have added a hard limit so no amount of buying can push a score past the
    maximum.
  - Score range is now 700 to 990, replacing 680 to 999.
  - No browsing without an account. Sign-up now comes before everything, CV upload
    included. This removed our riskiest piece of work and a data-protection concern.
  - Employers never see the real underlying score, even after paying to unlock.
    They see the same floored number the candidate sees. This closes a question that
    had been blocking us.
  - Revenue model rebuilt around subscriptions, courses, employer tiers and college
    seats, with admin control over seats and the ability to stop a college.
  - Duplicate CV flagging removed.
  - Employer type and industry fields added, waiting on your lists.
  - Reminder notifications added for people who sign up but never upload a CV.
  - Mock interview confirmed as recorded, not a live phone call.


================================================================================
ONE MORE THING YOU SHOULD KNOW
================================================================================

The changes above remove roughly a day and a half of work and add four to six days.
They also land on the hardest days in the schedule — the scoring engine, the unlock,
and payments — which were already the three days with no room in them.

We are not asking for anything here. We are telling you now rather than in week three
that the four-week timeline needs re-cutting against this new scope. We will come back
with a revised schedule once the blocking questions above are answered, because two of
them change how big the work actually is.
